ng diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-140454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44993624314b48147f859c371ebc2b814f7547ce32669e025d29143f55cb199f"} {"question" "q-140454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-140454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}14 & -25 \\\\ 20 & -36\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}14 & -36 \\\\ 20 & -25\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}20 & 36 \\\\ -25 & 14\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}25 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -20\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-140454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-97111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-97111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 18 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-97111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44993624314b48147f859c371ebc2b814f7547ce32669e025d29143f55cb199f"} {"question" "q-97111" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-97111" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 18 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 18 & -3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-97111" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 18 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-38362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-38362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-38362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference