\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-97281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/activi