 "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-140490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -11 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -13 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-140490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44993624314b48147f859c371ebc2b814f7547ce32669e025d29143f55cb199f"} {"question" "q-140490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-140490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -13 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}11 & 13 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}13 & -11 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -13 \\\\ 11 & 7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 13 & 11\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "q-140490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -13 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/state.json" "file_sha256" "436ec3446d638b7de18863d022545e96a0facaf2d1108f9fd91583e9b9917daa"} {"question" "e-8563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C},$ respectively. If $P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $\\mathcal{C}$ to basis $\\mathcal{B},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}\\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1}$\n\n- $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$\n\n- $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/example-8563.json" "file_sha256" "c3177842c37ec8a0299163939e6f05a9ec664392dc27a65ee2dedca967774084"} {"question" "e-8563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/2e82c232614bec8a98f079ea56d7913ee13e7b457e33e418198278d2e369fc5c.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/example-8563.json" "file_sha256" "c3177842c37ec8a0299163939e6f05a9ec664392dc27a65ee2dedca967774084"} {"question" "e-5516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/48ba234a07831383642525bf18fedcf6ea801576d80702e737d37334a04740e3.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/example-5516.json" "file_sha256" "39ba42c2d71031cfbae311c75a337702607f9a3346bbd80f47dd43b7939c53e8"} {"question" "e-5516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}.$ First, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/example-5516.json" "file_sha256" "39ba42c2d71031cfbae311c75a337702607f9a3346bbd80f47dd43b7939c53e8"} {"question" "e-5517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/fe3c67c0218808ba7b398d1659530d61a4c8ce3eafd196341ba6645ac7076f43.png)\n\nConsider the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/example-5517.json" "file_sha256" "977ce5edf6b224f9625e26020b6fc6f685bde8ddda13e32f79fe9a1bd7750504"} {"question" "e-5517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ and standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{S}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/d050ac7d04af79ad9b5e7f096e3d21c9808af4a756f43d46ca29beca00dedcc9.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-(-7)\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 13 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/example-5517.json" "file_sha256" "977ce5edf6b224f9625e26020b6fc6f685bde8ddda13e32f79fe9a1bd7750504"} {"question" "e-5897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/76bfd9209f632ac8576051154d7e4250d8c4c53ffe843238b6d25835704ce398.png)\n\nConsider the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, shown above. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/example-5897.json" "file_sha256" "3676d19cfea350e45eaca02cbf9c888b3d4c80a36ab716499ff1d69fc503d55f"} {"question" "e-5897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/cb0c35da5a4bd26c92343d7262efcb7ba7f197cdeea95c2d541b7441a6485804.png)\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/6759e7c098d5375894d540050e3c3dd9e3bb453b0cac7338b9b6754bb635f717.png)\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/example-5897.json" "file_sha256" "3676d19cfea350e45eaca02cbf9c888b3d4c80a36ab716499ff1d69fc503d55f"}] "usage" {"e-5897" [] "e-5517" [] "e-8563" [] "e-5516" [] "q-38362" [] "q-38356" []} "basis" 2235 "retractions" [[17592186129578 :question/problem "$[T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, P_{B → C} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{B → C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C$."] [17592186129580 :question/problem "$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}, [T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of $R^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S$."] [17592186128967 :question/problem "Let $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$, respectively. If $P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $\\mathcal{C}$ to basis $\\mathcal{B}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}\\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1}$\n2. $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$\n3. $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}}$"] [17592186128967 :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2014/Source/Images/f7d159aa878f5421b6005bd8da3c8d11.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$.\n\nSolving this equation for $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$, we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186128878 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$."] [17592186128878 :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2014/Source/Images/8e8b4e5e53d3ee1ee00e439e1bc6d2fe.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nOur goal is to compute $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}$. First, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{B → C}^{-1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\\\\n&= (1)/(1 \\cdot 1 - 0 \\cdot (-1))\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T]_{C} &= P_{B → C} \\cdot [T]_{B} \\cdot P_{B → C}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128879 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$."] [17592186128879 :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ and standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2014/Source/Images/16fb4c0100b25b8254f7b585766682f1.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{S}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}}^{-1}\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{B → S}^{-1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\\\\n&= (1)/(5 \\cdot 3 - (-7) \\cdot (-2))\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T]_{S} &= P_{B → S} \\cdot [T]_{B} \\cdot P_{B → S}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 13 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128908 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, shown above. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$."] [17592186128908 :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2014/Source/Images/c5e4593940236538bb9a20433d7b4e8d.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$. To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; c_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}c_{2} & b_{1}\\end{vmatrix}b_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1} \\mid:=R_{1} + (-1)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{B → C}^{-1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\\\\n&= (1)/(1 \\cdot 1 - 0 \\cdot (-1))\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T]_{C} &= P_{B → C} \\cdot [T]_{B} \\cdot P_{B → C}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186127263 17592186127264 17592186127265 17592186127266 17592186127267 17592186127275 17592186127276 17592186127277 17592186127278 17592186127279] "immutable_fields" [[17592186127262 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127262 :answer-field/id] [17592186127262 :answer-field/key] [17592186127262 :answer-field/type] [17592186127274 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127274 :answer-field/id] [17592186127274 :answer-field/key] [17592186127274 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
